1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,327
Hi — this is Bella, for Chaitanya.

2
00:00:02,367 --> 00:00:03,100
A bug report:

3
00:00:03,567 --> 00:00:05,833
the Logout button logs nobody out.

4
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:08,933
Every user, every time,
and only in production.

5
00:00:09,633 --> 00:00:13,660
This is the postmortem — what broke,
and why it was invisible where you'd

6
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:14,040
look.

7
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:15,573
Someone clicks Logout.

8
00:00:15,613 --> 00:00:16,947
The page redirects.

9
00:00:17,147 --> 00:00:18,447
And they're still signed in.

10
00:00:19,047 --> 00:00:23,173
Not a rare edge case, not a flaky one —
every time, for everyone, on the

11
00:00:23,213 --> 00:00:23,913
live site.

12
00:00:24,547 --> 00:00:27,113
The button does something;
it just doesn't do what it says.

13
00:00:27,430 --> 00:00:28,963
And here's what makes it interesting.

14
00:00:29,697 --> 00:00:33,397
The same code, on a developer's
laptop, logged out perfectly.

15
00:00:33,930 --> 00:00:37,497
It had worked for months, and nothing
in the logout code had changed.

16
00:00:37,930 --> 00:00:39,663
So before you read a line of it:

17
00:00:39,897 --> 00:00:42,097
whatever's wrong here
probably isn't the code.

18
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:44,113
On paper the button did a thorough job.

19
00:00:44,647 --> 00:00:47,213
It called the server to
record that you'd logged out.

20
00:00:47,747 --> 00:00:51,347
Then it walked every cookie it could
see and deleted it — plain domain,

21
00:00:52,047 --> 00:00:54,347
parent domain, localhost.

22
00:00:54,413 --> 00:00:58,613
Cleared local storage, cleared session
storage, and redirected you home.

23
00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:02,387
What it never did was ask the
identity provider to actually end the

24
00:01:02,427 --> 00:01:03,127
session.

25
00:01:03,293 --> 00:01:07,087
It cleaned up every piece of evidence
that you were logged in, by hand,

26
00:01:07,127 --> 00:01:09,260
and assumed that was the same thing.

27
00:01:09,727 --> 00:01:11,027
For months, it was.

28
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:14,707
On the development setup the session
cookie lived on the app's own domain.

29
00:01:15,573 --> 00:01:19,207
Page JavaScript could see it,
so deleting it by hand worked.

30
00:01:19,707 --> 00:01:23,340
On production the session lives on a
different domain — the provider's own

31
00:01:23,473 --> 00:01:26,207
— and it's marked HttpOnly.

32
00:01:26,307 --> 00:01:28,907
Page JavaScript cannot
read it or delete it.

33
00:01:29,273 --> 00:01:31,573
That's not a bug;
that's the point of the flag.

34
00:01:32,173 --> 00:01:34,840
It's what stops a malicious
script stealing your session.

35
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:39,387
So the loop ran, deleted everything
it could reach, reported success — and

36
00:01:39,427 --> 00:01:42,127
the one cookie that mattered
sat there untouched.

37
00:01:42,760 --> 00:01:45,527
Reload, and the provider
helpfully signs you back in.

38
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:47,227
The code wasn't wrong.

39
00:01:47,893 --> 00:01:50,093
Its assumption about where
the session lived was.

40
00:01:50,310 --> 00:01:51,977
The fix is one line in spirit:

41
00:01:52,443 --> 00:01:54,903
actually call the provider's sign-out.

42
00:01:54,943 --> 00:01:55,977
But look at how it's written.

43
00:01:56,677 --> 00:02:01,003
The call is raced against a five-second
timer, and if it loses, the code

44
00:02:01,043 --> 00:02:02,177
redirects you anyway.

45
00:02:02,943 --> 00:02:06,903
Signing out is a network call to
someone else's service — if that service

46
00:02:06,943 --> 00:02:10,737
is slow, you don't want a user
stranded on a page that won't let them

47
00:02:10,777 --> 00:02:11,280
leave.

48
00:02:11,320 --> 00:02:15,113
The cookie loop stayed, by the way —
it isn't what fixes this, but it's

49
00:02:15,153 --> 00:02:17,087
harmless and it covers the local case.

50
00:02:17,753 --> 00:02:19,687
And one thing is still live as I record.

51
00:02:20,353 --> 00:02:21,487
The fix is on main.

52
00:02:22,153 --> 00:02:25,713
The development branch does not have
it yet, so the next release cut from

53
00:02:25,753 --> 00:02:27,920
development would bring
the bug straight back.

54
00:02:28,190 --> 00:02:29,390
The Logout That Didn't.

55
00:02:29,823 --> 00:02:34,317
The full video has the root cause in
detail, the lesson, and a prompt you

56
00:02:34,357 --> 00:02:35,590
can run on your own app.

57
00:02:36,057 --> 00:02:37,223
The link is right below.
